"""pktrec-phy - bring-up tool for the Ethernet FMC Max (OP080) port subsystem of the
FPGA Packet Recorder: DP83867 PHY configuration over the fabric MDIO master, link
status, path selection and counters (M0), PHY SFD timestamping and the per-port
hardware traffic generator / checker (M2), TAP mode: per-pair mode, dynamic
inter-frame gap, link-state propagation and forwarding latency (M3).

Hardware (include/pktrec_regs.h):
  eth_ports/pktrec_eth_ports_0  0x4_2008_0000 (512 KB)  MAC/PCS regs, MDIO master, GT APB
  eth_ports/pktrec_m0_path_0    0x4_2002_0000 (4 KB)    PATH_CTRL, counters, PHY GPIO/PG,
                                                        traffic generator + checker (GEN_*/CHK_*)
  capture/pktrec_capture_0      0x4_2003_0000 (4 KB)    SFD timestamping (PKTREC_SFD_*, 0x400..)
All are bound to generic-uio in the device tree; the tool finds them by their
physical address in /sys/class/uio/uio*/maps/map0/addr (or uses /dev/mem with
--devmem). Dependency-free: mmap + memoryview (aligned 32-bit accesses) only.

Commands:
  init [--1000-only] [--reset] [--no-sfd] [--wait S]
                         configure all four PHYs (see init_phy) and re-trigger the
                         SGMII status word on every copper link that comes up
  status [--json]        per port: PHY link/speed/duplex, SGMII/PCS state, GPIO levels
  path <port|all> idle|loop|fwd|gen|replay [--i-accept-lan-loop]
                         select the TX source of a port (also keeps
                         PAIR_MODE consistent: a pair is "tap" when both ports are fwd).
                         replay (m0 map 1.3, M6) = path mode 4: frames from the replay
                         scheduler (pktrec-replay sets it itself); refused on a pair
                         that forwards (TAP), and fwd is refused next to a replay port.
                         Loop safety (see "Loop safety" below): loop is refused unless
                         the port's path is idle, its pair is not in TAP mode, its PHY
                         is not in loopback and it is not on the LAN pair; fwd/gen/
                         replay are refused into a port whose PHY is in loopback
  mode <pair 0|1> tap|monitor [--force] [--i-accept-lan-loop]   per-pair mode: tap =
                         both ports forward to each other (inline TAP), monitor = both
                         ports receive only.
                         Refused while a capture runs (capture CTRL.ENABLE) unless --force;
                         tap is refused while a port of the pair is in replay (--force
                         takes the port out of replay), and while a PHY of the pair is
                         in loopback (--force does not override that). In tap mode
                         pktrec-phy-watch (watch) propagates link loss across the pair
  safe [--json]          loop-safety report: per port PHY loopback / power-down, path,
                         effective path, pair mode, watch hold; flags every reflecting
                         combination (exit status 2 when one is found)
  test linkdown <port> [--i-accept-lan-loop] | test linkup <port>
                         simulate a link loss on a port with the safe method (the one
                         watch uses to hold a port): P1-P3 PHY power-down (BMCR[11]);
                         P0 (never powered down, see "P0 guard") path idle first, then
                         MII loopback. linkup undoes it (+ AN restart, SGMII re-trigger,
                         P0's fwd path restored if its pair is still in TAP mode).
                         Refused on the LAN pair (pair 0) unless --i-accept-lan-loop:
                         run link-propagation tests on pair 1 (P2<->P3)
  mode status [--json]   PAIR_MODE, PATH_CTRL and the effective path per pair
  ifg <port|all> [--enable on|off] [--nom B] [--min B] [--hi B] [--lo B] [--json]
                         dynamic inter-frame gap of the TAP forward path: settings,
                         FWD FIFO fill (now / max), reduced-gap time and entries, FLUSH
  latency (--pair P | --in A --out B) [--count N] [--timeout S] [--window NS] [--json]
                         TAP forwarding latency per frame, TX SFD(out) - RX SFD(in), from
                         the SFD log FIFOs while paced traffic flows (one frame in flight,
                         e.g. ping -s <size> -i 0.01 through the TAP): min/median/max
  counters [--clear] [--json]   M0 frame counters
  sfd status [--json] [--clear]   SFD timestamping: edges, matches, stale, mismatches,
                         MAC->SFD deltas, TX latency, latest timestamps per port
  sfd config [--rx-win MIN MAX] [--tx-win MIN MAX] [--rx-offset P NS] [--tx-offset P NS]
             [--match on|off] [--log on|off] [--min-low CYC] [--min-high CYC]
             [--mac-offset NS]   (MAC_OFFSET 0x0A8: ns added to MAC-fallback stamps;
                         persist all of these with 'pktrec-tb save-calibration')
  sfd log <port> [--dir rx|tx] [--json]   pop the port's SFD log FIFO (needs --log on)
  gen <port> start [--size B] [--count N] [--ifg B | --rate PPS] [--raw] [--etype E]
             [--dst MAC] [--src MAC] [--stream ID] [--seq-first N] [--rand-mask M]
             [--no-select] [--force] [--i-accept-lan-loop]   start the port's generator
                         (selects PATH gen unless --no-select); refused while the
                         port's PHY is in loopback (--force does not override that)
  gen <port> stop | status [--json]
  chk <port> start [--raw] [--etype E] | stop | status [--json]   receive checker
  (--size is the wire size INCLUDING the FCS, like test/gen_seq.py: 64..16387;
  size + rand mask must not exceed the port's MAC TX_MAX_LEN, 1518 B by default,
  or those frames go out error-terminated: refused unless --force)
  watch [--interval S] [--no-propagate] [--debounce S] [--grace S]
        [--no-0055-correction] [--skew-ns NS] [--json]
                         re-trigger the SGMII status word (CFG2[7] 0->1) whenever a
                         PHY's copper link changes (DP83867 sends it only once), and
                         for pairs in TAP mode propagate link state: when a port's
                         copper link stays down for --debounce s, hold its partner
                         with its copper link down, so the device on the other side
                         sees link down too: P1-P3 by PHY power-down (BMCR[11], MDI
                         off); P0, which must never be powered down (its CLK_OUT
                         clocks P1-P3, see "P0 guard"), by first setting path 0 idle
                         (EFF_PATH confirmed: nothing is forwarded into P0 any more)
                         and only then MII loopback (BMCR[14]). When the port comes
                         back, release: power-up / loopback cleared (+ AN restart),
                         SGMII re-trigger, BMCR re-check, and for P0 last of all path 0
                         fwd again. Held ports never propagate, and a released port
                         gets --grace s to negotiate before its link-down counts (no
                         ping-pong). On exit, or when the pair leaves TAP mode, every
                         held PHY is released.
                         On every copper link-up, read the PHY's skew FIFO status
                         (reg 0x0055): non-zero (0x0111 / 0x1011 seen on the 1000BASE-T
                         master) means this link-up has --skew-ns (8) ns more RX
                         latency, so the port's SFD RX_OFFSET is set to the calibration
                         base - 8 ns; zero restores the base (--no-0055-correction: off).
                         Every action is logged (--json: one JSON object per line);
                         state in $PKTREC_RUN_DIR/phy-watch.json (/run/pktrec).
  mdio <phy> <reg> [val] [--i-accept-lan-loop]   raw PHY register access (phy:
                         p0..p3 or MDIO address; reg > 0x1f uses the extended REGCR/
                         ADDAR indirection). A BMCR write with bit 14 (MII loopback)
                         set is checked against the loop-safety rule below

Loop safety: a PHY or path loopback on a port that is being forwarded into
sends the far side's traffic straight back at it. With a TAP pair between a
router and a host, MII loopback on the host-side PHY returned every frame from
the router to the router (P0 RX -> P1 TX -> PHY1 loopback -> P1 RX -> P0 TX):
the switch learnt every LAN address on the TAP port and the whole LAN went
down. Rule enforced here: a port may be put into a loopback (BMCR[14] via mdio,
path loop, test linkdown on P0) only if no path forwards into it (its own path
is idle and EFF_PATH confirms it) and it is not in a TAP pair; no non-idle path
(fwd/gen/replay, mode tap) is selected into a port whose PHY is in loopback; no
generator runs on a port whose PHY is in loopback. Pair 0 (P0/P1) is treated as
the LAN pair (PKTREC_LAN_PAIRS, default "0"; "" = none): path loop and test
linkdown are refused there too. --force never overrides these checks; only
--i-accept-lan-loop does, with a warning. The one internal exception is watch
holding P0, which reaches the safe state itself (path 0 idle first).

P0 guard: on the OP080 only P0's DP83867 has a crystal; its CLK_OUT (25 MHz)
feeds the XI clock input of P1-P3 (docs.opsero.com/op080/datasheet/
detailed-description/, "Clocks"). Powering P0 down (BMCR[11]) or changing its
CLK_OUT mode (IO_MUX_CFG 0x0170) stops that clock: P1-P3 lose their copper links
and stop answering MDIO (bench 2026-10-13: a power-down of P0 for link
propagation dead-locked the card). Every MDIO write goes through one function
that refuses both, for every command including 'mdio'.

There is one MDIO master for the four PHYs: every MDIO access (including the
4-step extended-register sequences and read-modify-writes) holds an flock on
/run/lock/pktrec-mdio, so 'watch' (pktrec-phy-watch.service) and CLI commands
never interleave on the bus.
"""

